MITRE ATT@CK Journey

tactics # 12 = Command & Control


🎯 Goal

The attacker is trying to communicate with the compromised system after gaining access.

Think of it like this:

🧑‍💻 Attacker → 💻 Victim Machine

The attacker needs a communication channel to:

Execute new commands
Download additional malware
Upload stolen information
Maintain remote control
Move to the next stage of the attack

Without C2, the attacker is like a person who breaks into a building but has no way to communicate with the team outside.


Common C2 Channels:
Attackers often disguise their traffic to look legitimate.

Examples include:

HTTP / HTTPS
DNS tunneling
PowerShell
Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP)
SSH
VPN tunnels
Cloud services (e.g., file-sharing or collaboration platforms)


                                                     [ very important ]

Windows Events & Telemetry

Unlike some tactics, there isn't a single Windows Event ID that says "This is C2." SOC analysts correlate multiple sources:

4688 – Process Creation

Suspicious processes:
powershell.exe
cmd.exe
curl.exe
certutil.exe
mshta.exe
wscript.exe

5156 – Windows Filtering Platform

Allowed network connections.

Sysmon Event ID 3 (if Sysmon is deployed)

Network connections made by a process.

Firewall, DNS, proxy, EDR, and SIEM logs are also critical for detecting C2.


Common Red Flags 🚩
PowerShell making outbound internet connections.
Communication with unknown or malicious IP addresses.
Periodic "beaconing" (regular outbound traffic every few seconds or minutes).
Long-running encrypted HTTPS sessions to suspicious domains.
Connections occurring outside business hours.


SOC Investigation Checklist
Whenever you detect possible C2 activity, ask:

✅ Who initiated the connection?
✅ Which process created it?
✅ What destination IP or domain was contacted?
✅ Is the destination known or malicious?
✅ How often is the connection repeated?
✅ Was malware executed before the connection?
✅ Was data uploaded or downloaded?
✅ Is the traffic encrypted?
✅ Does this match normal user behavior?


Real-World Example

An attacker compromises a workstation through phishing.

Then:

powershell.exe starts (4688).
The system connects to an unknown external IP (5156 or Sysmon 3).
Every 60 seconds, the workstation contacts the same IP (beaconing).
The attacker sends commands remotely.
Later, sensitive data is collected and prepared for exfiltration.

A SOC analyst correlates the process creation, network telemetry, DNS, firewall, and endpoint logs to identify the C2 channel.


